            "Jim Highsmith went further in 2009 with the Agile triangle, which puts value and quality at two corners and collapses scope, schedule and cost into a single \"constraints\" corner. His argument is that the goal is a valuable, releasable product; staying inside scope, schedule and cost is a boundary, not the measure of success.",
            "Be careful with the slogan \"Agile fixes time and cost and flexes scope\". It describes how many Agile teams plan, not a rule that applies to every Agile project. A regulated product may have scope that cannot flex at all, and then time or cost must give instead. Our comparison of [Agile vs Waterfall](/project-management/agile/agile-vs-waterfall/) explains how each approach handles a change in the triangle.",
